[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3646d9c7-a4bf-5f07-9367-56ce38c30d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\nM∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{17}{2}\\end{array}\\right],\n$$\n$\\,x=-1$ and $y=-\\frac{17}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285289} {:answer/id #uuid "85e90878-8e02-5e2b-907a-8de27d6e9a76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\nM∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right],\n$$\n$\\,x=-3$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286019} {:answer/id #uuid "c96d518f-8d36-5ee9-a22a-7b70c03b78d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\nM∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right],\n$$\n$\\,x=3$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286626} {:answer/id #uuid "d8aa53af-6559-5810-bd90-7bdc89bb1238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\nM∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{17}{2}\\end{array}\\right],\n$$\n$\\,x=\\frac{4}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286761} {:answer/id #uuid "ebff1f0e-b7a5-5d23-a4e1-84da86e0bfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\nM∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{9}{2}\\end{array}\\right],\n$$\n$\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=-\\frac{9}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e90878-8e02-5e2b-907a-8de27d6e9a76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\nM∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right],\n$$\n$\\,x=-3$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286019} :answer-field/id #uuid "4915c173-2020-5f73-a32b-91c5f3b7da26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33887d26-4540-5f7d-8312-2849fc1d5c71" :question/math-academy-id "q-20461" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 1 & -2 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-3\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{9}{2}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=-3$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0439511f-6bcb-5df1-b107-723ed7eac55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284822} {:answer/id #uuid "11540c92-a30f-5e29-852e-f44110b49f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284939} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd6e6c8-c066-57ed-aca5-d04d9fd31bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285371} {:answer/id #uuid "766a4044-6e95-5e6f-af50-99ef14df0af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285890} {:answer/id #uuid "d33d668e-b0ca-57c8-867a-44e3e4914dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0439511f-6bcb-5df1-b107-723ed7eac55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284822} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f7cd0e3-04ea-5f3a-b65d-50397f5fe917" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfd9b617-c165-5285-b91a-34485ed418b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-20907" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5,\\,y=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1),\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5))=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "042ce01d-341b-5a8f-b26c-2bae1091ffbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2{e}^{2t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284821} {:answer/id #uuid "085052cd-1f3e-5f31-9ff8-08a93c8512b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284852} {:answer/id #uuid "2277789f-926a-5240-a07d-b7aedc65a771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285096} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8848d4-7288-585c-b944-73aa40d45c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286635} {:answer/id #uuid "d0070ef7-3de9-5565-a190-4c165235a08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "085052cd-1f3e-5f31-9ff8-08a93c8512b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284852} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0b9c64d-9e93-55fe-a0fb-bcc5981e0b91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "809a8d30-1507-55ed-ad1d-5fa778afd784" :question/math-academy-id "q-20909" :question/problem "![](images/59/59704c6b8e643337113ab99b22eaeaac8d59ac3c15042409e8a0a8eac6ee54fd.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\frac{t}{2},\\,y={e}^{t}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{({e}^{2t})+\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7a49c5-c04a-5b09-961e-ce2173d168d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288127} {:answer/id #uuid "46970cdb-5477-540f-9132-ef9bdf11cf0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288516} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee94653-2683-539a-8752-9632bf820836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288695} {:answer/id #uuid "b412f0d6-a041-52a2-b716-a46e90405e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289221} {:answer/id #uuid "d43c814d-d944-5a23-9528-1d803515330d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d43c814d-d944-5a23-9528-1d803515330d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289437} :answer-field/id #uuid "70dda0c1-1de5-5b1a-9d65-3741d6332a87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72bd9c1b-8a89-551b-9ae2-b4bbe161eca6" :question/math-academy-id "q-21044" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=6{x}^{3}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =18{x}^{2}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 36\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}+1}{{x}^{4}}\\right) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{5}+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =36(-2)+\\frac{36}{(-2)^{4}}=-\\frac{279}{4}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =36(1)+\\frac{36}{(1)^{4}}=72>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acbe094-f343-5529-9ce7-737f0525b5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288114} {:answer/id #uuid "1c7651eb-94f4-5c1d-a7e6-63b85d680d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288214} {:answer/id #uuid "460eb23d-8842-59cc-87f1-d692d6507f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288509} {:answer/id #uuid "5adc34e2-26fc-5ef4-8b74-09b404a42a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288665} {:answer/id #uuid "da9664d7-9d8c-57e2-a405-167ff7d70fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da9664d7-9d8c-57e2-a405-167ff7d70fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289490} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fc7b2b7-86c9-59db-ae67-fadc002138c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47254316-6f9b-52c5-92f7-d2e76625d399" :question/math-academy-id "q-21047" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=8{x}^{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =16x-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) & =16-\\frac{2}{{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{3}}=-112<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =16-\\frac{2}{(1)^{3}}=14>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is indeed a point of inflection."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d51796-1e90-516a-8924-e9533734bb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288109} {:answer/id #uuid "36db837b-33cb-5d96-8b2f-d3e83d2b633a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288400} {:answer/id #uuid "54f508a8-907b-55ac-a56a-cf18ffcf74a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288620} {:answer/id #uuid "de28db8b-3981-5e3e-a256-6a38f6ef9f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289509} {:answer/id #uuid "e86f3bd1-1f61-5565-86f0-519521398255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09d51796-1e90-516a-8924-e9533734bb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288109} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f458853-e94e-50ff-b421-b1b9e47c556b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a399dc0-bc45-559e-a9f3-72a30ed1d856" :question/math-academy-id "q-210662" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-2.7<X<2.7)=0.4108,$ find $\\sigma .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.7<X<2.7) & =0.4108 \\\\ 2P(0<X<2.7) & =0.4108 \\\\ P(0<X<2.7) & =0.2054 \\\\ P(X<2.7)-P(X<0) & =0.2054 \\\\ P(X<2.7)-0.5 & =0.2054 \\\\ P(X<2.7) & =0.7054 \\\\ P(X\\le 2.7) & =0.7054\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.7) & =0.7054 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.7-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.7054 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{2.7}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.7054 \\\\ \\frac{2.7}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7054)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.54)=0.7054\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7054)=0.54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.7}{\\sigma } & =0.54 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2.7}{0.54} \\\\ \\sigma  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093fcc1f-b272-5206-9f10-d3ff9b4e7ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288102} {:answer/id #uuid "4701c588-7a64-5da7-b605-bd2098872ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288521} {:answer/id #uuid "58088476-f3ba-57c3-9404-13aa28971826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288646} {:answer/id #uuid "642d250f-4219-5be1-a098-8d5442e93773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288739} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a9f2d2-6c41-51e4-8304-9113b94277a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "642d250f-4219-5be1-a098-8d5442e93773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288739} :answer-field/id #uuid "78df4f50-4474-567c-bf4e-a9d66c503caa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f113d04-8735-5974-90c6-4d33ae43ead3" :question/math-academy-id "q-210769" :question/problem "If the distance between the points $(b-2,2b)$ and $(1,-1)$ is minimized, then $b=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & =(b-2-1)^{2}+{(2b-(-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(b-3)^{2}+(2b+1)^{2} \\\\  & ={b}^{2}-6b+9+4{b}^{2}+4b+1 \\\\  & =5{b}^{2}-2b+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =10b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 10b-2 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)=10>0.$ Therefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{1}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f204513-0c1c-5316-9773-1e20fbfaedf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288231} {:answer/id #uuid "8dab9274-9e7f-5d69-ab82-2c603dccac03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288980} {:answer/id #uuid "92fc4a84-947c-50ea-9a48-19c2ebdae226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289017} {:answer/id #uuid "ab82b0ca-0fa4-58d6-8780-bef43cc778c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289167} {:answer/id #uuid "d66e0c76-ceac-5494-add6-8ce67226b514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab82b0ca-0fa4-58d6-8780-bef43cc778c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289167} :answer-field/id #uuid "68a75d09-c131-5ef6-a123-eddff3b06ce5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ae50ba9-b72d-560f-8e00-a86ce7d78e7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-210779" :question/problem "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(a,2a-1)$ and $(4,-2),$ where $a$ is a real number?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & ={(a-4)}^{2}+{(2a-1-(-2))}^{2} \\\\  & =(a-4)^{2}+(2a+1)^{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}-8a+16+4{a}^{2}+4a+1 \\\\  & =5{a}^{2}-4a+17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =10a-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 10a-4 & =0 \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)=10>0.$ So, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=\\frac{2}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(a)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $a=\\frac{2}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2}-4\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)+17} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{81}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c5f668-99cc-57ae-9aa1-c42826108073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288184} {:answer/id #uuid "198ad89c-6e8f-5eae-9cfe-e55c408928c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288195} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbd5568-6840-5618-99ef-43df7bb7a7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288217} {:answer/id #uuid "90e67383-12a4-5bfc-b46d-0b975463386c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289003} {:answer/id #uuid "978d403e-cf60-5347-a7d6-7c079d3aaddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cbd5568-6840-5618-99ef-43df7bb7a7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288217} :answer-field/id #uuid "17bd9008-7bf4-5734-b9a7-fdab0718b66f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e8dbfeb-f356-5014-87e7-43786a605372" :question/math-academy-id "q-211194" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}+2$ that is closest to the point $(3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{x-2}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+x-2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1008f91c-5312-57ff-b584-94eee41fe74d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)-2{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288152} {:answer/id #uuid "1da60212-9b1b-5f90-b4ad-60a5d028fa99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)-4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288219} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0f56b4-96f6-5e10-b8c1-e96fcb0adb3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288328} {:answer/id #uuid "c400b002-970a-5f9c-b980-7c4fcc67cff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289327} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b052e5-81af-5157-9a69-881e064e72dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b052e5-81af-5157-9a69-881e064e72dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289641} :answer-field/id #uuid "92070a66-23e2-56dd-9cc9-c4b8a5e22afc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6e26edb-f404-57aa-9e8d-fef8eb7ecc78" :question/math-academy-id "q-21339" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d3de1db-0fcc-5d54-a34d-c4772f63450c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288331} {:answer/id #uuid "4d6783bc-60cb-5ebf-aed7-e258b2417e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288571} {:answer/id #uuid "72dafc8f-1319-50ac-8575-b91418fddf5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+3{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288830} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd6ac01-4a42-5fe6-a775-9f769a7e8298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+3{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288884} {:answer/id #uuid "8bef632c-9e9e-5375-8c17-7ff1526dfe34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bef632c-9e9e-5375-8c17-7ff1526dfe34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288967} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cb71b35-5d29-5e3f-a2c7-defaa9483e1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97f4a57c-d78d-5eaa-81d0-22dc3ac7dd99" :question/math-academy-id "q-21347" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\nfrom $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69475fb9-4639-5aee-af40-ad859a263a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288774} {:answer/id #uuid "91284f31-5e28-57a6-85bb-2b392b818d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289008} {:answer/id #uuid "a96e4ea4-d995-5d4a-b26e-2d37c11a1916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289155} {:answer/id #uuid "ac501491-2831-575c-ac89-af59b5aa9473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289176} {:answer/id #uuid "ef891aab-5620-5243-8798-2c0bd3ae8835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac501491-2831-575c-ac89-af59b5aa9473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289176} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e6962ab-6088-50b4-b289-81a33c131c7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1524414a-eeb0-5a99-93da-15745003f6ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-21354" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y=t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}+(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c7f0634-61fd-5c82-88da-7e629117634a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450182} {:answer/id #uuid "899d8517-fc94-54d3-9ecb-f90896d7de46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "899d8517-fc94-54d3-9ecb-f90896d7de46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450183} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec9d5e27-2edf-590b-89a2-5622f244e8c1" :answer-field/key "$x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451045} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c3829bd-23f7-5558-ad2d-ad25aa7aa625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450184} {:answer/id #uuid "f83818b6-de85-5ed1-a6ea-fd4bc327eefa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f83818b6-de85-5ed1-a6ea-fd4bc327eefa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450185} :answer-field/id #uuid "7522a75c-221f-5f7e-ba8d-0b0b878f71a2" :answer-field/key "$x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aac2952-926a-57ab-ba80-e57e635b4afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450186} {:answer/id #uuid "43d49d7f-c711-5838-b3c3-f67c7b4e70b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aac2952-926a-57ab-ba80-e57e635b4afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450186} :answer-field/id #uuid "58d5a316-4f26-5f0a-a784-27dddbbf543e" :answer-field/key "$x=0$ is a local minimum" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a70a3cbd-ee1f-54d1-83af-a484013d0e65" :question/math-academy-id "q-21468" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+9,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=16{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=4{x}^{2}(4x+3).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(4x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,0.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,-\\frac{1}{2},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =4(-1)^{2}(4(-1)+3)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =4{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}(4\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+3)=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4(1)^{2}(4+3)=28>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{4})$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $(-\\frac{3}{4},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{3}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum”; “$x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a173d2bb-a6d0-55c0-ad6c-1a58c9ab8da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450212} {:answer/id #uuid "949115a8-52b2-59e7-a41c-27c119276413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "949115a8-52b2-59e7-a41c-27c119276413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450213} :answer-field/id #uuid "44ca7820-6d41-551d-a79b-65f6050bd2e4" :answer-field/key "${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{5})<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451060} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b01ddae9-b073-55fa-ae34-870a6f063c6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450214} {:answer/id #uuid "37a6e913-689c-58e3-97b3-b21ca71ab6c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b01ddae9-b073-55fa-ae34-870a6f063c6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450214} :answer-field/id #uuid "24a3d678-7c46-5db8-b51c-5df7c09f1740" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=\\frac{1}{5}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451061} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c94ee2d-3972-57b4-bf31-f0632abdace0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450216} {:answer/id #uuid "65dd89d8-e402-5203-96cf-3c51d35a911d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65dd89d8-e402-5203-96cf-3c51d35a911d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450217} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f065d4b-9a26-5fcb-b19f-3bc754c9ebea" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=\\frac{1}{5}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f189f1ac-fb45-57eb-b495-98b85aa3c2fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-21541" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-2{x}^{5/3}+{x}^{2/3}.$ Given that ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\frac{1}{5}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=-2{x}^{5/3}+{x}^{2/3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2/3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-1/3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-\\frac{20}{9}{x}^{-1/3}-\\frac{2}{9}{x}^{-4/3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right) & =-\\frac{20}{9}\\cdot {5}^{1/3}-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {5}^{4/3}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {5}^{1/3}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)<0$; “$f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=\\frac{1}{5}$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbe9c7df-3baf-502a-bdf1-650e5114f4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbe9c7df-3baf-502a-bdf1-650e5114f4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289694} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bee18c5-a61f-5ef1-8b3d-563d9d708f2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac5a3f61-2785-57a6-8ba3-6a2018ca7d25" :question/math-academy-id "q-215939" :question/problem "The lines $y=2x+1$ and $kx-10y=3$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=2x+1$ is $2.$\n\n- To find the slope of $kx-10y=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-10y & =3 \\\\ 10y & =kx-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{10}x-\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $kx-10y=3$ is $\\frac{k}{10}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{k}{10}\\right)=-1\\,⟹\\,k=-5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80e55783-b575-58b9-a332-72a7c4d966f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80e55783-b575-58b9-a332-72a7c4d966f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288901} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ff07bfd-cbbf-59c2-b98c-cd9fdfb57c50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "136056a6-37b6-586a-94ae-13431344c9a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-215941" :question/problem "The lines $y=-5x+3$ and $3kx-5y=9$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=-5x+3$ is $-5.$\n\n- To find the slope of $3kx-5y=9,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3kx-5y & =9 \\\\ 5y & =3kx-9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3k}{5}x-\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $3kx-5y=9$ is $\\frac{3k}{5}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-5)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3k}{5}\\right)=-1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "476917e8-859c-5503-88ab-ca2e9ff2c81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "476917e8-859c-5503-88ab-ca2e9ff2c81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288522} :answer-field/id #uuid "c91da3c3-adba-5714-9611-6c6417b39dc2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07f8e54a-3bdb-557d-bce5-8db99b8641ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-215942" :question/problem "The lines $y=7x-2$ and $kx-14y=7$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=7x-2$ is $7.$\n\n- To find the slope of $kx-14y=7,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-14y & =7 \\\\ 14y & =kx-7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{14}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $kx-14y=7$ is $\\frac{k}{14}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n7\\cdot \\left(\\frac{k}{14}\\right)=-1\\,⟹\\,k=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbe7ea72-6463-5c20-ac8b-5b88d1211278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbe7ea72-6463-5c20-ac8b-5b88d1211278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289385} :answer-field/id #uuid "356372fc-bfc0-50b3-9743-019b3d7fa2cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289847} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59243eec-6a69-5df0-ad4a-8597490742a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59243eec-6a69-5df0-ad4a-8597490742a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288656} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b555aad-2fc1-5a0d-a348-ab90e2e21186" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e767452a-c701-5541-89cb-42716f9405f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-216066" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{6}v-\\frac{1}{6}w &=2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}v+\\frac{1}{5}w &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$v=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,w=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $6$ and the second equation by $5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{6}v-\\frac{1}{6}w &=2 && |\\times 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}v+\\frac{1}{5}w &=2 && |\\times 5\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4v-w &=12 \\\\ 2v+w &=10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the second equation can easily be solved for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v+w & =10 \\\\ w & =10-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $w=10-2v$ into the first equation and then solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4v-w & =12 \\\\ 4v-(10-2v) & =12 \\\\ 4v-10+2v & =12 \\\\ 6v & =22 \\\\ v & =\\frac{22}{6} \\\\ v & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $w,$ we substitute $v=\\frac{11}{3}$ into the equation $w=10-2v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =10-2v\\text{ } \\\\  & =10-2\\left(\\frac{11}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10-\\frac{22}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-22}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $v=\\frac{11}{3}$ and $w=\\frac{8}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23dab659-478f-52b4-8055-ea2c11f88490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55\\pi }{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288258} {:answer/id #uuid "31aa8e00-eb62-5e6e-937b-282c9a340667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288357} {:answer/id #uuid "594bb244-0365-535d-925f-f19583be5d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi }{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288657} {:answer/id #uuid "a2473559-5a3e-581d-88f3-e7c6c6db867b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36{\\pi }^{2}}{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289116} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1ca394-42ff-5d5b-979a-bea2e38fdc6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36\\pi }{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31aa8e00-eb62-5e6e-937b-282c9a340667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288357} :answer-field/id #uuid "c767c10d-0804-5409-92ca-a7f83886135e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4e5366d-cdd2-5bf4-9255-f48b21270169" :question/math-academy-id "q-21607" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=x+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{11\\pi }{6}=\\frac{55{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ecd06ce-ff7a-5c63-80d7-6791d3b60b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288229} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f509c0-ef53-5451-a927-e1535f79d5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $8$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289242} {:answer/id #uuid "bb098e6e-7cc7-5cbe-97e9-d1c2e9d1b7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $9$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289266} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f1dd6c-eee7-5523-ac21-571a49e81cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $10$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289541} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb8be69-dba7-5d19-ad05-28bbf89224ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $11$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ecd06ce-ff7a-5c63-80d7-6791d3b60b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288229} :answer-field/id #uuid "735be538-bc1e-5645-9fa7-56422609d0dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f64f9f3-48d9-568b-881f-f163dc09ba3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-218498" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,08$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n256,\\,64,\\,16,\\,4,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=256$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{64}{256}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =256\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{4}\\cdot {4}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={4}^{5-n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,08.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,08 \\\\ {4}^{5-n} & <0.000\\,08 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{5-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08) \\\\ (5-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08) \\\\ 5-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}-5 \\\\ n & >5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}≈11.805\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,08.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c14db59a-5903-5eb2-a881-001d6a5d5e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c14db59a-5903-5eb2-a881-001d6a5d5e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289311} :answer-field/id #uuid "02af0bbe-51a6-5d5a-8c08-cd4c322fac91" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289734} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1f1c3a8-e3fd-557c-98d8-6af928d2c7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1f1c3a8-e3fd-557c-98d8-6af928d2c7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289113} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2b726d-3532-55e2-ab3b-c21e3a83a5cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289793} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2207dc9-eda4-56d0-ba73-d6dcca1b8315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2207dc9-eda4-56d0-ba73-d6dcca1b8315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289316} :answer-field/id #uuid "61042d02-bc0e-5fb3-8749-0a4f5cca1d61" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b993c3c-e8bb-5de5-8511-a2d9d6d2b174" :question/math-academy-id "q-218759" :question/problem "Rewrite $-8{x}^{2}+32x-29$ by completing the square.\n\n$-8{x}^{2}+32x-29=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8$. So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{29}{8}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}-\\,{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}) & =-8({(x-2)}^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3-8{(x-2)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42535c72-7dca-5dec-b3ff-5fffdca01f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450238} {:answer/id #uuid "a723ba80-925c-5c6f-b824-4657e3763b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a723ba80-925c-5c6f-b824-4657e3763b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450239} :answer-field/id #uuid "927229ca-cec9-54ee-84e6-e8567db7f670" :answer-field/key "${f}^{″}(1)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451073} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34f5f5ee-9d41-5697-860a-23a36e8dc208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450240} {:answer/id #uuid "0320d12f-354d-5a2f-9b06-77916089c851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34f5f5ee-9d41-5697-860a-23a36e8dc208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450240} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a715903-5d8f-5eb3-95f3-4d0b210e7a9b" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451074} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e97d5d-53e2-5191-b2a3-5fe8ef8805b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450242} {:answer/id #uuid "1a9e1351-94e4-59a7-a354-ac7fe35e350e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91e97d5d-53e2-5191-b2a3-5fe8ef8805b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450242} :answer-field/id #uuid "449efc8e-51a4-5cbe-91df-a44cc22f3849" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "691aeb79-c22f-5658-8744-f63c6c0d8d61" :question/math-academy-id "q-22040" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3/2}-12{x}^{1/2}-6{x}^{-1/2}$ defined on $(0,\\infty ).$ Given that ${f}^{′}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3/2}-12{x}^{1/2}-6{x}^{-1/2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{1/2}-6{x}^{-1/2}+3{x}^{-3/2}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+3{x}^{-3/2}-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{-5/2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {1}^{-1/2}+3\\cdot {1}^{-3/2}-\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot {1}^{-5/2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{1/2}-6{x}^{-1/2}+3{x}^{-3/2} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{-3/2}(x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point on $(0,\\infty )$ is $x=1.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=\\frac{1}{2},2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =3{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-3/2}{(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =3{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-3/2}{(\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right))}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{1}{{2}^{-3/2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{3/2}\\cdot {2}^{-2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{-1/2}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =3\\cdot {2}^{-3/2}(2-1)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{-3/2}>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=1$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${f}^{″}(1)=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1857e73d-9e93-573d-86c0-691ba8252055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450274} {:answer/id #uuid "15eab213-03a9-5c29-bce8-49592113ad29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15eab213-03a9-5c29-bce8-49592113ad29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450275} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1cee12c-3988-5479-adc2-2248b173b2ca" :answer-field/key "${f}^{″}(1)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451091} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9425f38-0674-5f5c-8f60-b52d212afaf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450276} {:answer/id #uuid "44f74282-34b1-5c08-8ddb-fc4cc9a96af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9425f38-0674-5f5c-8f60-b52d212afaf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450276} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffa20abb-07bb-54e0-bf7e-c0428b1062a1" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0efce9bd-67c4-5882-a57a-5cb3b2b6160d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450278} {:answer/id #uuid "c105de90-0b62-567a-92ae-13f29dbbc1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0efce9bd-67c4-5882-a57a-5cb3b2b6160d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450278} :answer-field/id #uuid "43e7da68-627e-5b97-9277-da153955c2b9" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80042f1e-abb0-596e-b942-cf69eb1639aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-22084" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{4/3}-24{x}^{1/3}-6{x}^{-2/3}$ defined on $(0,\\infty ).$ Given that ${f}^{′}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=3{x}^{4/3}-24{x}^{1/3}-6{x}^{-2/3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{1/3}-8{x}^{-2/3}+4{x}^{-5/3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{-2/3}+\\frac{16}{3}{x}^{-5/3}-\\frac{20}{3}{x}^{-8/3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot (1)^{-2/3}+\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot (1)^{-5/3}-\\frac{20}{3}\\cdot (1)^{-8/3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find the stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{1/3}-8{x}^{-2/3}+4{x}^{-5/3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{-5/3}(4{x}^{2}-8x+4) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{-5/3}{(x-1)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point on $(0,\\infty )$ is $x=1.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=\\frac{1}{2},2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =4{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-5/3}{(\\frac{1}{2}-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =4{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-5/3}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{1}{{2}^{-5/3}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{5/3}\\cdot {2}^{-2} \\\\  & ={2}^{5/3}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\cdot {2}^{-5/3}(2-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2-5/3} \\\\  & ={2}^{1/3}>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=1$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${f}^{″}(1)=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad3e0aa-0ecb-5b45-b73c-497f51eb1f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ad3e0aa-0ecb-5b45-b73c-497f51eb1f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288314} :answer-field/id #uuid "90cbc86e-fd80-52ed-b920-292039fdb1b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a455dece-e051-505c-99b8-692017ed3883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a455dece-e051-505c-99b8-692017ed3883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289128} :answer-field/id #uuid "eba984db-e836-5a2f-a0af-85e0fe8023c1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d1d4ff1-0f32-51b3-b771-45a49de2ebf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-221409" :question/problem "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}-17x+{\\left(\\frac{17}{2}\\right)}^{2})+5=(x\\,\\,-$\n{{field-1}} $)^{2}+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {x}^{2}-17x+{\\left(\\frac{17}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{17}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{17}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {(x-\\frac{17}{2})}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& ({x}^{2}-17x+{\\left(\\frac{17}{2}\\right)}^{2})+5 \\\\\n&= {(x-\\frac{17}{2})}^{2}+5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae52e0ed-00b0-50da-8715-9d81b53ea499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450304} {:answer/id #uuid "df2e3b01-ed68-5e5a-9fd7-f06fd9e21549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df2e3b01-ed68-5e5a-9fd7-f06fd9e21549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450305} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb63b29-214a-5b89-b7d7-991c0a721a03" :answer-field/key "${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2})=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451106} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a84b8c5-2064-532a-9a2f-6557205825b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450306} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e6f721-c1f3-5184-9e84-23c29756475a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a84b8c5-2064-532a-9a2f-6557205825b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450306} :answer-field/id #uuid "66a3d71e-7a37-5b3d-91c3-6edb8711278a" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1cec0ec-cb5e-5476-82bf-e6c40ae1a8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450308} {:answer/id #uuid "5216dfe6-c770-51bb-b512-f6b5ce7278b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1cec0ec-cb5e-5476-82bf-e6c40ae1a8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450308} :answer-field/id #uuid "a381e2d7-52e0-55d3-8f4e-7a0fc4550846" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "daf4ce47-bbe8-55cf-a5e8-b43d87274625" :question/math-academy-id "q-22187" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+3x+3.$ Given that ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+3x+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{2}-12x+3=3(2x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=24x-12.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =24\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=0,1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =3(2\\cdot 0-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(2\\cdot 1-1)^{2}=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d617821-6685-519e-9a4d-28281ac958a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288333} {:answer/id #uuid "099d7c1b-7772-5cc6-9777-a7e269acac3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "099d7c1b-7772-5cc6-9777-a7e269acac3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483587} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ee93b5a-7a7f-5de6-b120-52987f2cfbac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "705b4f99-555b-54e9-8531-2285040b4156" :question/math-academy-id "q-223101" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,k).\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$k$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "674f313a-77f0-5fc8-abb7-91c3e1a7184e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288758} {:answer/id #uuid "999808ca-0444-5335-ac02-8d182fdfd0a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "999808ca-0444-5335-ac02-8d182fdfd0a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442585} :answer-field/id #uuid "88606f6e-77e8-504e-bf1e-6d51d3bd6834" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0891613-fa7a-56c3-a9d8-1b98ea108f48" :question/math-academy-id "q-223107" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,k$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n$j$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}\\,t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k \\\\  \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\  \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4961a14-388a-5b76-9063-6584b2a0778a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4961a14-388a-5b76-9063-6584b2a0778a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289644} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cef406a-ea9f-5a4c-8a84-bd5cc907ac0d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08b660d8-2de0-57eb-af5f-98c5287529ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-223114" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3t,\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,0\\,,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3,\\,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3,\\,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{5},\\,-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{4}{5}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e53c6fa-b88e-5996-a931-7e9fb5cf482f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e53c6fa-b88e-5996-a931-7e9fb5cf482f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289096} :answer-field/id #uuid "23309c45-ebdb-528a-84a6-ba2fae3cf57a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289814} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba1f5e84-6542-55f0-b2b5-226edc295377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba1f5e84-6542-55f0-b2b5-226edc295377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289260} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b63762a-9e11-51e9-b0a9-fd55097541a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6800959f-090f-5fdb-a727-120e768644ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-223560" :question/problem "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{81}{4})+2=(y\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}} $)^{2}+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2ay+{a}^{2}=(y+a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{81}{4} \\\\\n&= {y}^{2}+9y+{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& ({y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{81}{4})+2 \\\\\n&= {(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5aefa84-cbe2-57d3-a7a2-fd784b217046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5aefa84-cbe2-57d3-a7a2-fd784b217046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289555} :answer-field/id #uuid "cabb38b6-1a0f-57a3-b72b-19039258e861" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290171} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caba86a1-e7e2-5362-8cca-fbdd59c2f18c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caba86a1-e7e2-5362-8cca-fbdd59c2f18c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289377} :answer-field/id #uuid "cda6ce23-21ce-5504-bbad-ef62a064a046" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd82d6cc-a4d8-5108-b016-979bbf922e63" :question/math-academy-id "q-223562" :question/problem "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({u}^{2}-3u+\\frac{9}{4})+5=(u\\,\\,-$\n{{field-1}} $)^{2}+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{u}^{2}-2au+{a}^{2}=(u-a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {u}^{2}-3u+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\\n&= {u}^{2}-3u+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {u}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}u+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {(u-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& ({u}^{2}-3u+\\frac{9}{4})+5 \\\\\n&= {(u-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7cca